## ARCHITECTURE — EVERY FEATURE NEEDS ALL THESE LAYERS
```
backend/src/
├── models/FeatureName.ts                    ← Sequelize model (database shape)
├── services/featureName.service.ts          ← ALL business logic lives here
├── controllers/featureName.controller.ts    ← ONLY handles req/res, calls service
├── routes/featureName.routes.ts             ← Express router, mounts controller
└── types/featureName.types.ts               ← TypeScript interfaces for this feature
```

Rule: Controllers must never contain business logic.
A controller should only: validate input → call service → send response → catch errors.

---

## COMMENTING RULES — MANDATORY ON EVERY FILE
The developer presents this code at their university defense.
Every file MUST have thorough comments. No exceptions.

### RULE 1 — File header (top of every file):
```typescript
/**
 * @file filename.ts
 * @description What this file does in one or two plain English sentences.
 *              Explain its role in the system, not just what it contains.
 */
```

### RULE 2 — JSDoc on every function and method:
```typescript
/**
 * @description Plain English: what this function does and WHY it exists
 * @param {Type} paramName - What this parameter represents and why it is needed
 * @returns {Type} What is returned and what the caller does with it
 * @throws {Error} When this fails and under what conditions
 * @example
 * const token = generateToken(user.id);
 */
```

### RULE 3 — Inline comments explain WHY, not WHAT:
```typescript
// GOOD — explains the reasoning behind the decision
const saltRounds = 10; // 10 rounds is the OWASP-recommended minimum for bcrypt

// BAD — just restates what the code already shows
const saltRounds = 10; // set saltRounds to 10
```

### RULE 4 — Section dividers group related lines:
```typescript
// ─── 1. Validate Input ────────────────────────────────────────
// ─── 2. Check Database ────────────────────────────────────────
// ─── 3. Hash Password ─────────────────────────────────────────
// ─── 4. Save to DB ────────────────────────────────────────────
// ─── 5. Return Response ───────────────────────────────────────
```

### RULE 5 — Flag important logic with emoji markers:
```typescript
// SOFT LOCK: Marks this file as being edited to prevent conflicts
// WARNING: This must run inside a DB transaction — race condition risk
// NOTE: Token expires in 7 days, set by JWT_EXPIRES_IN in .env
// AUTH: Requires a valid JWT in the Authorization: Bearer header
// FILE: Stores to local disk in dev, switches to S3 in production
// CRON: This job runs every 30 minutes to clean up expired locks
```

### RULE 6 — Comment every database query:
```typescript
// Find all files locked for more than 2 hours.
// This protects against files being permanently locked if a
// user's browser crashes before they could manually unlock.
const staleFiles = await File.findAll({
  where: {
    isBeingEdited: true,       // only look at currently locked files
    editingStartedAt: {
      [Op.lt]: twoHoursAgo     // locked more than 2 hours ago
    }
  }
});
```

---

## THE SIGNATURE FEATURE — SOFT FILE LOCKING
This is the most important and unique feature of CloudTeams.
Always treat it as first-class. Always comment it thoroughly.

How it works:
1. User opens file → POST /api/files/:id/lock
   → sets isBeingEdited=true, editedBy=userId, editingStartedAt=NOW()
2. Other users see: "Alice is currently editing this file"
3. Every 30 seconds → POST /api/files/:id/heartbeat keeps the lock alive
4. User closes file → POST /api/files/:id/unlock → clears the lock
5. Cron job every 30 min → auto-releases locks older than 2 hours (crash safety)

Database columns on the files table:
  is_being_edited    BOOLEAN DEFAULT FALSE     -- is someone currently editing?
  edited_by          INTEGER REFERENCES users  -- who has the lock?
  editing_started_at TIMESTAMP                 -- used to detect expired locks

---

## STANDARD API RESPONSE FORMAT
Always return this shape:

Success:
  { success: true, message: 'Optional message', data: { payload } }

Error:
  { success: false, error: 'ErrorName', message: 'What went wrong' }

